  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Messages:\n\n* Critical Fire Weather Conditions across Northern Rockies, Saturday \n  afternoon through Saturday night.\n\n* Choppy wave activity on area lakes for Saturday afternoon and \n  evening.\n\n* Relatively cool airmass in place Sunday through early next week, \n  but summer warmth returns thereafter.\n\nThe warm ridge of high pressure currently over the Northern Rockies \nwill provide a few more days of hot afternoon temperatures. \nAnticipate most low elevations warming into the 90s and 100s (F). \nDespite these temperatures being in line with recent trends, \nremember to take take frequent breaks, drink plenty of water, seek \nshade, and never leaving any person or pet in a parked vehicle. \nThere is a 70 percent probability of showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping across Lemhi County Idaho and portions of southwest \nMontana this evening.\n\nThe ridge of high pressure is anticipated to break down Saturday \nthrough Sunday, resulting in gusty winds and low relative humidity \nacross the Northern Rockies. Red Flag conditions will develop at \nmany locations. Those recreating on area lakes, particularly \nFlathead Lake, will experience choppy wave activity Saturday \nafternoon through late evening. A cold front is expected to sweep \nacross the Northern Rockies Saturday evening.\n\nA relatively cool airmass is projected to rush over the Northern \nRockies Saturday night through Sunday. Gusty winds will return \nduring daytime Sunday and once again increase wave activity on area \nlakes. However, the cooler daytime temperatures should keep \nafternoon humidity a bit higher compared to Saturday.\n\nPrepare for very efficient radiational cooling across the Northern \nRockies overnight Sunday. High mountain valleys will cool to the 30s \nin many locations by Monday morning.\n\nLong range ensembles generally agree that a warm high pressure ridge \nwill return to the Northern Rockies around the middle of next week. \nModels also tend to agree that the next iteration of our summertime \nridge will be several degrees cooler than the current ridge.",
